  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q18-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "18",
    "prompt": "Suppose there are just two types of people: knights, who always tell the truth, and knaves, who always lie.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p19-q18a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Two people, Ben and Cara, stand before you. Ben says “Cara is a knave.” Cara says “Ben and I are both knights.” What are they really?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Ben is a knight; Cara is a knave.",
          "worked": "Suppose Ben is a knave. Ben's statement is false, so Cara is a knight. Cara's statement must then be true, making Ben a knight, a contradiction. Thus Ben is a knight, so Cara is a knave. Cara's claim that both are knights is false, as required.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 42
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q18b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Two people, Ben and Cara, stand before you. Ben says, “We are both knaves.” What are they really?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Ben is a knave; Cara is a knight.",
          "worked": "Suppose Ben is a knight. Ben's statement would be true, making Ben a knave, a contradiction. Thus Ben is a knave and the statement that both are knaves is false. Since Ben is a knave, Cara must be a knight.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q18c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Two people, Ben and Cara, stand before you. Ben says “Cara is a knave”. Cara says “Neither Ben nor I are knaves.” What are they really?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Ben is a knight; Cara is a knave.",
          "worked": "Suppose Ben is a knave. Ben's statement is false, so Cara is a knight. Cara's statement must be true, so neither person is a knave. This contradicts the assumption about Ben. Therefore Ben is a knight and Cara is a knave. Cara's statement is then false, as required.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 42
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q18d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Three people, Amelia, Ben and Cara, stand before you. Cara says “Three of us are knights”. Ben says “Two of us are knights”. Amelia says “One of us is a knight”. What are they really?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Either Amelia is a knight and Ben and Cara are knaves, or all three are knaves.\n\nIn the first arrangement, only Amelia's statement is true; in the second, all three statements are false.",
          "worked": "Suppose Cara is a knight. All three would be knights, but Ben's and Amelia's statements would be false. This is a contradiction, so Cara is a knave.\n\nSuppose Ben is a knight. There would be exactly two knights, so Amelia would also be a knight. Amelia's statement would then be false, a contradiction. Hence Ben is a knave.\n\nSuppose Amelia is also a knave. There would be no knights, making Ben's statement that there are two knights and Cara's statement that there are three knights false, but Amelia's false statement would require the number of knights to differ from one. Although this alone is consistent with zero knights, it shows that the all-knave case also satisfies every statement being false.\n\nThus there are two possible arrangements: Amelia is a knight and Ben and Cara are knaves; or all three are knaves.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 100
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q18e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Now for something harder. You encounter two doors, guarded by Alice and Ben: one who always tells the truth and one who always lies. You do not know which door leads to safety and which to danger. By asking one guard just one question, prove that it is possible to determine which door is safe.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Ask either guard: “Which door would the other guard say leads to safety?” Choose the other door.\n\nEither guard identifies the dangerous door.",
          "worked": "Ask either guard: “Which door would the other guard say leads to safety?”\n\nIf the guard is truthful, the guard truthfully reports the liar's answer, which identifies the dangerous door.\n\nIf the guard is the liar, the other guard would identify the safe door. The liar gives the opposite answer, identifying the dangerous door.\n\nIn either case, choose the door that the guard does not identify.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      }
    ]
  },
